hey this is pressure Locker you have a  fair coin that shows heads 50% of the  time and tails 50% of the time you and a  friend play a coin flipping game can you  create a rule classifying the coin flips  so that you win with probability exactly  equal to P and your friend wins with  probability exactly equal to one minus P  the probability can be any rational or  irrational number between 0 &amp; 1 the game  has to end in a finite number of coin  flips with probability 1 can you figure  it out you can also try this extension  to the problem now you have a coin that  shows heads with probability Q between 0  &amp; 1  but unfortunately the value Q is unknown  to you in other words you have a biased  coin but you don't know the bias can you  now figure out a game where you win with  probability P the value of P is known to  you the same restrictions apply the game  has to end in a finite number of coin  flips with probability 1 can you figure  out these problems give them a try and  when you're ready keep watching the  video for the solution let's get started  with some examples imagine you wanted to  win a game with probability 3/4 one way  to do this is you could imagine flipping  the coin 2 times this generates four  equally likely sequences of coin tosses  we can classify three of those sequences  as a win for you and one of those  sequences as a loss for you this will be  a game in which you win 3/4 of the time  now imagine instead you wanted to win a  game with probability 1/3 we can  slightly modify this procedure we have 4  coin tosses what we do is we class  if I wanted them as a win two of them as  a loss and we say the third sequence two  tails in row we can just classify that  as disregard these two tosses and start  all over so flip two times again and we  again have the same rules on what you do  with the coin tosses you will win this  game with probability 1/3 we can  generalize this procedure imagine you  wanted to win a game with probability X  over Y you would need to flip the coin  just enough times so that you get to a  value that's more than Y we can then  classify X of the sequences as a win we  can classify Y minus X of the sequences  as a loss and then we can classify the  rest of the sequences as starting over  just disregard them and start over  according to the same rules you will win  this game with the probability of x over  y but what if you wanted to win a game  with probability one divided by PI now  since 1 over pi is irrational we cannot  write it exactly as x over y for  integers X and y so this procedure is  not going to be easily modified to  figure out things where we need an  irrational probability so we're going to  use a different procedure the solution  is to use binary numbers so we'll write  the probability P in terms of its binary  expansion so this will be a sequence of  fractions that are powers of two this  can be written as zero point b1 b2 b3  and so on so just like we write decimal  numbers these are going to be fractional  numbers that are in binary  each of these fractional numbers b1 b2  these represent powers of 1/2 so how is  this going to be useful for us  I'll give some examples of binary  numbers so we have the fraction 1/2 this  can be written as 1/2 plus 0 over 2 to  the power of 2 plus 0 over 2 to the  power of 3 and so on so in binary form  this will be 0 point 1 and then there  will be zeros after that the number one  third will be written as point zero one  zero one  repeating it'll be 0 1 0 1 and so on 1  over pi also will have a binary  expansion this will go on forever but  there is a binary expansion to this  number so how is this going to help us  we have the probability in its binary  form here's the game we're going to  define flip a coin until it lands on  heads for the first time let's say  that's toss number n the rule of the  game is that if B sub n equals 1 you win  the game if B sub n equals 0 then you  lose the game so for example let's say  the probability is 1/3 this will be 0 1  0 1 and so on you win if the first time  the coin shows heads is on an even  number toss 2 4 6 et cetera and you lose  the game otherwise so how come this game  has a probability of 1/3 of you winning  the game let's prove that it always has  a probability of P the probability you  win the game is the probability that the  first heads shows on toss number K times  the value of the binary digit in the  value K so the probability that you get  the first heads showing on toss K is 1  over 2 to the power of K and the  probability that the binary digit is  equal to 1 in that spot is exactly the  binary digit because the binary digit in  spot K is either going to be 0 or 1  what we have is we have come up with  exactly the binary representation of P  therefore the probability you win this  game is exactly equal to P so there's  another way you can use binary numbers  to create a game in which you win with  probability P we start out with our  binary representation of P we now do  something slightly different for each  flip of the coin we record a 1 if we  flipped a heads and we record a 0 if we  flipped tails we are going to keep  flipping the coin as long as flip K  equals the binary digit of K once we get  to a point where the flip differs say  that's toss n we're going to define that  you win the game if your flip was tails  and you're going to lose the game if the  flip was heads so why does this  procedure work let's go through the  example where P is equal to 1/3 we have  our binary representation so here's how  the game would work let's imagine we  flip tails heads heads the first two  digits 0 1 are the same as the 0 1 in  1/3 but that third value in 1/3 is equal  to 0 and we flipped a heads on our last  one which was different so we're going  to stop flipping after this third flip  and the last flip we had was a heads so  we differed and it was a heads so we're  going to define this as a loss if  instead we flipped tails tails that  would mean our second toss was different  from the binary expansion of 1/3 and in  this case the last flip differed as the  tails so in this case we would win the  game so this may sound like a strange  procedure but there's an intuitive way  to see why  works intuitively the coin flips will  define a random number Z that's between  0 &amp; 1 if this number Z that we get to by  flipping ends up being less than P we  are winning the game and if it's more  than P we lose the game so basically  we're creating with our coin flips a  random number between 0 &amp; 1 and if this  number ends up being in the interval 0  to P we're going to win  so we win this game with probability P  here's a graphical way to see what's  going on in this game we start out with  our interval 0 1 let's suppose we have a  probability of P of 1/3 so we're saying  the winning interval is numbers that end  up between 0 and 1/3  now the first toss of our coin suppose  it's a heads so we know that our number  Z is going to start out with 0.1 well a  number that starts out 0.1 is going to  define something between 1/2 and 1 it's  going to at least be 1/2 because 0.1  means we're adding 1/2 to whatever  remains so if our first toss is a heads  we're going to end up in something  that's larger than one-third and there's  no way that we could end up in the  interval between 0 and 1/3 so our number  differs in the very first spot and it  differs to be larger so we're going to  lose the game for sure let's suppose we  continued where the first toss was the  tails this instead would define a number  between 0 and 1/2 we can never get a  number larger than 1/2 because the first  spot in our expansion of Z is equal to  point zero so in order to see where Z  ends up we're going to have to flip  again so let's say at this point we flip  tails so a point 0 0 so now we're going  to define an interval that's actually  between 0  1/4 Z has to be less than 1/4 from its  form furthermore we have a number Z or  that second digit 0 0 that's differing  from the second digit of P but in this  case the differing digit is a tails so  we know that we are going to win this  game for sure to give one more example  suppose that we had tosses of tails  heads heads now the first time we  differed was the third toss however we  differed by being a heads so we actually  ended up in an interval that's larger  than one-third so we're going to lose  this game for sure so now let's tackle  the extension we have a biased coin that  shows with probability heads with  probability Q how can we solve the game  now what we're going to do is we're  going to make a fair toss from the  biased coin we're going to flip the  biased coin two times this creates four  possible different tosses we don't know  the probability of each of these tosses  but we're going to classify them as  follows we're going to say if you toss  two heads or two tails just disregard  that outcome and start over if however  you tossed heads tails classify that as  a heads and if you clap tossed tails  heads classify that as a tails so while  we don't know the exact probability of  these outcomes we can calculate their  theoretical probability because we know  the coin shows heads with probability Q  so if we wanted so the probability of  heads will be tossing a heads followed  by a tails so this will be Q times 1  minus Q the probability of tossing a  tails will be the probability of tossing  T followed by H so this will be 1 minus  Q times Q what you'll notice here is  that the probability of HT is exactly  equal to the probability of th  in other words we have a game which ends  with heads or tails exactly 50% of the  time for each of those two cases the  probability of heads is equal to the  probability of tails in other words we  have made a fair coin out of a biased  coin so now the problem reduces so what  do we do if we have a fair coin and we  need to win the game with probability P  well we've just solved that problem we  can use one of the binary number  procedures that have just been described  and we can get to a game which we win  with probability P so it's an absolutely  stunning result we can simulate any  probability P that we want rational or  irrational from a coin and it can be a  biased coin where we don't even know the  bias did you figure out this problem  thanks for watching this video please  subscribe to my channel I make videos on  math and game theory you can catch from  my blog mind your decisions you can  follow on Facebook Google+ and patreon  you can catch me on social media at  pressure Locker and if you like this  video please check out my books there  are links in the video description
 

